#!markdown

# Exact fractions and repeating periods

This notebook keeps `1/10` as the exact rational `1/10`, then asks for a binary
representation. The repeated block is metadata, not a rounded `decimal` value.

#!csharp

#r "nuget: NumeralSystems.Net, 5.1.0"
using NumeralSystems.Net;

var options = new NumeralConversionOptions(
    256,
    NumeralRoundingMode.ToNearestEven,
    detectRepeatingPeriod: true,
    InfiniteExpansionBehavior.PreservePeriod);

var oneTenth = new RationalValue(1, 10);
var binary = oneTenth.Expand(2, options);
new {
    Text = binary.ToString(NumeralAlphabet.Base2),
    binary.RepeatingStartIndex,
    binary.RepeatingLength,
    Exact = binary.Value.ToString()
}

#!markdown

Compare bases. A denominator terminates exactly when all of its reduced prime
factors also divide the destination base.

#!csharp

Enumerable.Range(2, 19)
    .Select(denominator => {
        var expansion = new RationalValue(1, denominator).Expand(10, options);
        return new {
            Denominator = denominator,
            expansion.IsTerminating,
            expansion.RepeatingLength
        };
    })
